Routers need IP addresses for every network they connect to )every interface has its ip).

IPv4 is 32 bits long (4 bytes). They can be divided into 4 class:
A -> First octet is 0XXXXXXX and first octet numeric range 0-127 -> we usually consider the range finishing at 126 instead of 127, since anything in the range
127.0.0.0 - 127.255.255.255 is a loopback address and will always come back to the local host
B -> First octet is 10XXXXXX and first octet numeric range 128-191
C -> First octet is 110XXXXX and first octet numeric range 192-223
D -> First octet is 1110XXXX and first octet numeric range 224-239
E -> First octet is 1111XXXX and first octet numeric range 240-255

We will be using only class A, B, and C which are respectively /8, /16/, and /24

Netmask: it's another form of writing / in the IP address:
A -> 255.0.0.0
B -> 255.255.0.0
C -> 255.255.255.0

If the host portion of the IPv4 address is all 0's, it means it the network address. If the host portion is 255, it's the broadcast address.

To see the different interfaces on cisco router (in priviledge exec mode): show ip interface brief then we can configure the ip address of the interface with ip 
address <ip> <subnet>. We then have to change the state to up using: no shutdown

Router interfaces have the shutdown command applied by default, which means that it will have the administratively down/down state by default.
